{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OPERATOR DECISION (2026-08-07): CLEAN BREAK — no alias.\n\n`--force-close` is renamed to `--force` on transition outright. No compatibility alias ships. Rationale matches the epic-2f79 precedent for retiring `reconcile`: a stale caller should fail loudly with an unknown-flag error rather than silently continuing to work, and an alias would keep both spellings alive in help, docs and muscle memory indefinitely.\n\nImplementation implications:\n- every in-repo call site and doc reference migrates in the SAME change (search for the string, not just the symbol — CLI flags appear in docs, tests, skills and prompt text);\n- argparse must reject `--force-close` with a clear error naming the new spelling, NOT silently abbreviation-match it. Note 424f is setting `allow_abbrev=False` for exactly this class of hazard;\n- CHANGELOG entry required, since this is user-visible and breaking."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786096928110754001,"uuid":"44178481-4ac5-4348-a23b-21613d586c08"}